# CHECKOUT — design notes (parked)

Status: discussion only, no code. Park until real requirements
(booking_requirements option list, Stripe account/integration
status) are known.

## Core concept

A shared, event-parameterized checkout funnel. Every event page has
a call-to-action button (label varies — "Buy Tickets", "RSVP", "I'm
Interested", etc.) that, depending on the event's booking type,
either routes to one shared checkout entry point or takes a
different, non-payment action.

Single-item only — one event per checkout, no cart, no quantity/
session state (at least for v1; see "future" note below). This is
NOT a shopping cart. It's closer to "this button click carries one
piece of context (which event) forward" than a retail cart pattern.

## Driving field: booking_requirements (BR)

New/renamed ACF field (was tentatively `attendance_type`, rename to
`booking_requirements`). Controlled vocabulary (Select/Radio, not
free text), options TBD but likely:

- `ticketed` — routes to the shared checkout, which then hands off
  to Stripe. Label default: "Buy Tickets".
- `rsvp` — separate, non-payment action (e.g. a form, mailto).
  Label default: "RSVP".
- `enquire` — separate, non-payment action. Label default: "I'm
  Interested".
- `free` — no CTA button at all. Free entry, just turn up.

BR determines TWO things, not one:
1. Button label (via a PHP lookup array, centrally controlled —
   not a free-text field per event, to keep wording consistent
   across the site without relying on staff retyping it correctly
   each time).
2. URL/routing behaviour — for `ticketed`, the URL is CONSTRUCTED
   by code (e.g. checkout.example.com/?event_id=40), not typed by
   staff. For `rsvp`/`enquire`, likely still a manually-entered
   link field (external form, mailto) — TBD whether these need
   their own sub-logic too.

## Overlap to resolve when this is picked back up

`booking_required` (existing boolean field) and the new BR field
ask overlapping questions. Likely BR should REPLACE
`booking_required` entirely, since `free` in the new vocabulary
already covers "no booking needed" — worth deciding when built,
not carrying two fields expressing the same idea.

## Architecture — where this should live in code

This is NOT just a rendering/display concern (unlike everything
else in main_display.php so far) — once URL behaviour is involved,
it's transactional/business logic. Should live in its own
function, or its own small file, not inline in main_display.php's
render block.

## Build approach: custom PHP, not a plugin — for the routing/UI layer

Reasoning:
- Visual consistency — any WordPress booking/checkout plugin ships
  its own opinionated markup, near-guaranteed to clash with Forty's
  matched design language. Fighting plugin CSS costs more than
  building the simple thing directly.
- Scope match — plugins solve the general case (multi-item,
  inventory, tax, shipping); this needs one narrow flow (one event,
  one Stripe session). Using a general tool for a narrow job means
  carrying unused complexity and update/security burden.
- Fits the site's established pattern — everything built so far
  (menus, shell, tiles, ACF wiring) is precisely-scoped custom PHP
  matching Forty's own vocabulary. A third-party plugin would be
  the first real black-box dependency in an otherwise fully
  understood codebase.

EXCEPTION — the actual payment/Stripe layer itself should NOT be
hand-rolled. Use Stripe's own official PHP SDK or hosted Checkout /
Payment Links, not a WordPress marketplace plugin. WordPress should
never handle card data directly (keeps the site out of PCI scope).
Custom PHP = the form/routing/button layer only. Stripe's own
tooling = the actual money-handling layer.

## Known context / background

Site previously had THREE separate Stripe integrations (one per
something — presumably per event type or per old page). This
redesign consolidates to one shared checkout entry point,
parameterized by event, rather than duplicated per-event/per-page
integrations.

